KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ pair<const int,int> is_constructible from pair<int,int>&&: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [meta.unary.prop], [temp.deduct.call], [temp.arg]: is_constructible
<pair<const int,int>, pair<int,int>&&> is true (g++/clang++ agree).  CBMC reports
false: the C++17 converting move constructor pair(pair<U1,U2>&&) (stl_pair.h:708)
is gated by an __enable_if_t<...> non-type parameter.  Deduction CORRECTLY binds
U1=U2=int (template_map holds ::651::_U1->int, ::651::_U2->int), but
template_mapt::apply does not substitute those bound parameters inside the
`ambiguous` condition expression of the enable_if parameter type, so the
references survive as bare cpp_names, typecheck_type throws, and the candidate is
dropped ("found no match for symbol 'pair'").  user_defined_conversion_sequence
correctly delegates the template converting constructor to new_temporary; the
defect is the substitution gap in template_mapt::apply.  Root of std::map /
std::unordered_map insert(_Pair&&) overload selection for a convertible pair
(dog-food: expr.cpp, expr_util.cpp, pointer_predicates.cpp,
irep_serialization.cpp).  Reproduces only with libstdc++'s full pair machinery
(a hand-written enable_if<...>::type form substitutes correctly), hence the
header dependency.  Flip to CORE once template_mapt::apply substitutes deduced
parameters inside an enable_if non-type-parameter condition.
